==English==
{{was wotd|2010|June|6}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|xno|en}} {{m|fro|redunder}}, {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|redonder}}, and their source, {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|rēdundō}}, from {{m|la|rē}} + {{m|la|undō||surge}}, from {{m|la|unda||a wave}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈdaʊnd/|/ɹəˈdaʊnd/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-redound.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aʊnd}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To [[swell]] up (of water, waves etc.); to [[overflow]], to [[surge]] (of bodily fluids). {{defdate|14th-19th c.}}
#* '''1596''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''{{w|The Faerie Queene}}'', IV.10:
#*: For every dram of hony therein found / A pound of gall doth over it '''redound''' […].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[contribute]] {{m|en|to}} an [[advantage]] or [[disadvantage]] for someone or something. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* '''a. 1729''', {{w|John Rogers}}, ''A prudent cobduct recommended and enforced''
#*: The honour done to our religion ultimately '''redounds''' to God, the author of it.
#* '''1970''', Alvin Toffler, ''Future Shock'', Bantam Books, p. 448:
#*: The fact that in one case the advance '''redounds''' to private advantage and in the other, theoretically, to the public good, does not alter the core assumptions common to both.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[contribute]] {{m|en|to}} the honour, shame etc. of a person or organisation. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* '''2008''', {{w|Peter Preston}}, ''The Observer'', 2 Mar 2008:
#*: One thing about the 'John McCain-didn't-sleep-with-a-lobbyist' story '''redounds''' to the New York Times' credit.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[reverberate]], to [[echo]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[reflect]] (honour, shame etc.) {{m|en|to}} or {{m|en|onto}} someone. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To attach, come back, accrue {{m|en|to}} someone; to reflect back {{m|en|on}} or {{m|en|upon}} someone (of honour, shame etc.). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: ''His infamous behaviour only '''redounded''' back upon him when he was caught.''
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[arise]] {{m|en|from}} or {{m|en|out of}} something. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a wave, flood, etc.}} To [[roll]] back; to be [[sent]] or [[driven]] back.
#* {{RQ:Milton PL|book=7|passage=The evil, soon driven back, '''redounded''' as a flood on those from whom it sprung.}}

